<item>
  <title>Survival Analysis: Understanding the Kaplan-Meier Estimator</title>
  <dc:creator>Cheng-Han Yang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://cyang728.github.io/posts/2026-02-02-kaplan-meier/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<section id="introduction" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>Survival analysis is a branch of statistics that deals with analyzing the time until an event of interest occurs. The <strong>Kaplan-Meier (KM) estimator</strong> is one of the most fundamental and widely used tools in this field. In this post, I’ll walk through the motivation, mathematics, and intuition behind this elegant method.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-challenge-censored-data" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-challenge-censored-data">The Challenge: Censored Data</h2>
<p>Our goal is to estimate the <strong>survival function</strong>: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AS(t)%20=%20P(T%20%3E%20t)%0A"> which tracks the probability of remaining event-free over time.</p>
<p>The main difficulty arises from <strong>right-censoring</strong>: we often lose track of subjects before they experience the event (e.g., due to study termination or withdrawal).</p>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-important callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
Censoring is Information, Not Failure
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>A censored observation is not a failure. It provides the invaluable information that the subject survived <em>at least until</em> their censoring time. Methods that simply ignore this partial information will incorrectly bias survival estimates downward.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Therefore, a valid method must use information from <strong>both</strong> events and censored observations.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-flaw-in-the-naive-approach" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-flaw-in-the-naive-approach">The Flaw in the Naive Approach</h2>
<p>Consider a survival dataset with times in months: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A6,%5C%207,%5C%209%5E+,%5C%2010,%5C%2012%5E+,%5C%2015%20%5Cquad(%5Ctext%7B+%20indicates%20censoring%7D)%0A"></p>
<section id="a-naive-calculation" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-naive-calculation">A Naive Calculation</h3>
<p>What’s the survival probability past 11 months, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S(11)">? A simple empirical proportion would be: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cwidehat%7BS%7D_%7B%5Ctext%7Bnaive%7D%7D(11)%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Ctext%7BNumber%20of%20subjects%20observed%20beyond%2011%20months%7D%7D%7B%5Ctext%7BTotal%20number%20of%20subjects%7D%7D%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B2%7D%7B6%7D%20%5Capprox%200.333%0A"> (This only counts the subjects at times <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?12%5E+"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?15">.)</p>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-warning callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
Problem
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>This is <strong>wrong</strong> because it mishandles censored data. The subject censored at 9 months is known to have survived for at least 9 months. This naive method effectively treats them as if they failed to survive past 11 months, which is incorrect.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="notation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="notation">Notation</h2>
<p>For subject <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?i%20=%201,%20%5Cdots,%20n">:</p>
<ul>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?T_i">: true event time (possibly unobserved)</li>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?C_i">: censoring time</li>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y_i%20=%20%5Cmin(T_i,%20C_i)">: observed time</li>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cdelta_i%20=%20%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(T_i%20%5Cle%20C_i)">: event indicator</li>
</ul>
<p>Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t_%7B(1)%7D%20%3C%20%5Ccdots%20%3C%20t_%7B(J)%7D"> be the <strong>distinct event times</strong>. At each <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t_%7B(j)%7D">:</p>
<ul>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n_j%20=%20%5C#%5C%7B%5Ctext%7Bat%20risk%20just%20before%20%7D%20t_%7B(j)%7D%5C%7D"></li>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?d_j%20=%20%5C#%5C%7B%5Ctext%7Bevents%20at%20%7D%20t_%7B(j)%7D%5C%7D%20=%20%5C#%5C%7Bi:%20%5Cdelta_i%20=%201,%20Y_i%20=%20t_%7B(j)%7D%5C%7D"></li>
<li><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?c_j%20=%20%5C#%5C%7B%5Ctext%7Bcensored%20during%20the%20interval%20%7D%20%5Bt_%7B(j)%7D,%20t_%7B(j+1)%7D)%5C%7D"></li>
</ul>
<p>We take <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S(t)%20=%20%5CPr(T%20%3E%20t)"> (right-continuous) and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S(t%5E-)%20=%20%5CPr(T%20%5Cge%20t)"> (left limit).</p>
</section>
<section id="the-kaplan-meier-estimator" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-kaplan-meier-estimator">The Kaplan-Meier Estimator</h2>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-note callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
Definition
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>Assuming <strong>noninformative censoring</strong> (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?T%20%5Cperp%20C">), the survival function can be estimated by: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cboxed%7B%5Cwidehat%7BS%7D(t)%20=%20%5Cprod_%7Bj:%20t_%7B(j)%7D%20%5Cle%20t%7D%20%5Cleft(1%20-%20%5Cfrac%7Bd_j%7D%7Bn_j%7D%5Cright)%7D%0A"></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="key-properties" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="key-properties">Key Properties</h3>
<ul>
<li>The KM estimator <strong>drops only at event times</strong></li>
<li>Censored observations reduce later risk sets but do not cause drops</li>
<li>It produces a characteristic <strong>step-function</strong> plot</li>
<li>The most critical assumption is <strong>noninformative censoring</strong></li>
<li>The curve’s precision decreases as the number of subjects at risk diminishes over time</li>
</ul>
</section>
</section>
